[38;5;241m[48;5;255m❯ [38;5;16mWithout using any tools, write a 400 word story about a lighthouse.[39m

[38;5;16m[49m●[39m The lighthouse on Kestrel Point had not gone dark in ninety-one years, and Maren meant to keep it that way.

  She inherited the keeper's job from her grandfather, who inherited it from a man whose name was carved above the door and worn nearly smooth. Every evening she climbed the one hundred and twelve iron steps,
  trimmed the wick, polished the brass, and wound the clockwork that turned the great lens. The light swept out over the black water and the rocks below, a slow white arm that told ships where the land ended.

  Most nights nobody saw it but the gulls. The fishing boats from the village had long since moved to the bigger harbor down the coast, and the radio beacons on the steel towers did the navigating now. The
  lighthouse was kept for the sake of keeping it, and Maren knew how that sounded to other people.

  In late November a storm came up from the south with no warning the radio bothered to give. By dusk the sea was throwing spray higher than the gallery windows. The lamp's motor coughed once, and the beam
  stuttered.

  Maren ran. She knew the clockwork better than the motor, so she took the stairs two at a time and reached the lantern room with her lungs burning. The weight cable had slipped its drum. She had no spare cable, no
  tools that fit, and no time to go down for them. So she wrapped her scarf around the drum, braced her shoulder against the frame, and turned the crank by hand, counting the revolutions the way her grandfather had
  taught her, so the beam would keep its pace.

  Hours went by. Her hands bled through the wool. Somewhere past midnight a horn sounded far out, then another, then a third, and she understood that the boats were not lost after all. They had seen the light and
  were feeling their way toward it.

  At dawn the storm broke. The sea lay down, gray and tired, and the morning sun caught the glass.

  Three trawlers came in past the rocks that morning, their crews standing on the decks and staring up at the tower. Maren did not wave. She sat on the cold floor of the lantern room with her back against the lens
  housing and watched the boats pass under her light, one after another, until the last one was safely in the lee of the point.

  Then she climbed down, one hundred and twelve steps, and went to find a new cable.

